No—not the actual SAT Lagrangian from your framework.

What I have in this conversation is only the material you provided: the definition \(B=3/(4\pi)\), the proposed mass law, the \(B^5\) relation, and references to torsion, braiding, projection, and lattice stabilization. You haven't supplied the Lagrangian itself.

I don't want to invent one and then pretend the resulting \(B_{\rm eff}\) is a derivation.

If you have the SAT paper/manuscript, **paste or upload the section containing the Lagrangian** (ideally also the definitions of the fields and coupling constants). Then I can work forward from it:

\[
\mathcal L_{\rm SAT}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{field equations}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{projection solution at the relevant scale}
\;\longrightarrow\;
B_{\rm eff}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\frac{3}{2B_{\rm eff}^{5}},
\]

with the proton/electron ratio kept completely out of the calculation until the final comparison.

If the Lagrangian is in a PDF, you can upload the PDF directly and I'll extract the relevant equations.
